<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sveriges Riksbank</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1668">1668</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Riksbanken</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sveriges Riksbank is the central bank of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, founded in 1668. It is the oldest central bank still operating. It contributed to the development of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-bank">central banking</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sveriges-riksbank.jpg" alt="Sveriges Riksbank" width="320" height="256" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arild Vågen · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Riksbankshuset_April_2015.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Before the Riksbank, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> was served by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholms-banco">Stockholms Banco</a>, founded by Johan Palmstruch in 1656. Although the bank was private, the king chose its management. It collapsed because it issued too many notes without the necessary collateral, and Palmstruch, held responsible for its losses, was condemned to death but later received clemency.</p><p>On 17 September 1668 his privilege to operate a bank was transferred to the Riksens Ständers Bank, the Bank of the Estates of the Realm. Because of the failure of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholms-banco">Stockholms Banco</a> it was placed under the direct control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riksdag-of-the-estates">Riksdag of the Estates</a>, to keep the king from interfering. It took the name Sveriges Riksbank when a new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riksdag">Riksdag</a> was instituted in 1866.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Having learned from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholms-banco">Stockholms Banco</a>, the Riksbank was not permitted to issue bank-notes, although in 1701 permission was given to issue credit-notes. When counterfeit notes appeared in the middle of the 18th century, it founded its own paper-mill, Tumba Bruk, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a>. The first commercial banks were also allowed to issue notes, on condition of a deposit at the Riksbank. Through the 19th century the Riksbank was dominant as a credit institution and note issuer, and opened its first branch office in 1824.</p><p>Its position as a central bank dates from 1897, when the first Riksbank Act gave it the exclusive right to issue bank-notes, after repeated demands that private banks stop issuing them. The currency was backed by gold until 1931, when a temporary law freed the bank from the duty to exchange notes for gold; the law was renewed yearly until the constitution of 1975 made the bank a stand-alone organisation.</p><p>In November 1992 the fixed exchange rate of the krona collapsed, and in January 1993 the bank adopted a floating exchange rate and an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> target, with help from the Bank of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>. On 2 July 2009 it became the first central bank to set a negative interest rate, in response to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/2008-financial-crisis">2008 financial crisis</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Riksbank is regarded as the world's oldest surviving central bank. After its third centenary in 1968 it instituted the annual Sveriges Riksbank Prize in Economic Sciences in Memory of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-nobel">Alfred Nobel</a>, awarded with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prizes</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a> on 10 December. Its motto is Hinc robur et securitas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> for strength and security from this. In the 2000s it closed all its branches in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and put the handling of coins and notes in private hands, and by 2023 it had completed a technical pilot of an e-krona without deciding whether to issue one.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sveriges Riksbank” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Riksbank</b>, founded in 1668, is the oldest central bank still in operation, and its predecessor Stockholms Banco issued Europe’s first banknotes in 1661.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sweden">Kingdom of Sweden · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Stockholms Banco issued Europe's first banknotes in 1661, and its successor, the <b>Sveriges Riksbank</b> of 1668, is the oldest central bank still operating.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
